    "heading": "Warming Up With Some Simple Sums",
    "body": "Starting off the day with the easy grid was a breath of fresh air. I immediately looked for the smallest regions to get some quick wins. The single cell at [0,1] had a sum target of 6, which meant that cell had to be a 6. Since [0,1] was part of a domino with [0,0], I looked at my list and saw the [6,0] domino. This worked perfectly because the sum of [0,0] and [1,0] needed to be 6, and with [0,0] being a 0, [1,0] had to be 6. \n\nNext, I tackled the large equals region at [2,0], [3,0], and [3,1]. This told me all three cells had to share the same value. Looking at my dominoes, the only way to fill [3,0] and [3,1] with the same number using one domino was to use the double [3,3]. That meant [2,0] also had to be 3. From there, the rest of the pieces just fell into place. I used the [3,6] domino for [2,0] and [1,0], and the [3,0] domino for [1,2] and [1,3], which satisfied the empty constraint at [1,3]. It felt so satisfying to see the whole grid click together with that final [2,6] domino in the bottom right.",
