  "hard": {
    "heading": "The Big Eleven Breakthrough",
    "body": "Wow, the hard puzzle really lived up to its name today! I spent a good five minutes just staring at the layout before I found my way in. The breakthrough came from the sum of 11 at [2,0] and [3,0]. In the world of dominoes, there is only one way to get an 11 pips sum: a 5 and a 6. I looked at my remaining pieces and saw the [6,5] domino, which fit there perfectly. That gave me the anchor I needed to start branching out into the rest of the board.\n\nAnother tough section was the equals region involving [2,5], [2,6], [3,5], and [3,6]. Having four cells tied together like that limits your options significantly. I had to manage the [2,5] and [4,6] dominoes carefully to make sure the values matched up across the regions. I hit a dead end when I tried putting the [0,0] domino in the wrong spot, but once I realized that the empty constraint at [1,0] was the only place for a 0, everything smoothed out. It was a complex dance of moving pieces, but seeing that final sum of 6 at [0,7] and [1,7] balance out was the best part of my morning.",
